    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Often called the first global empire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ruled lands across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Asia from the 1490s.</li><li>American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from mines like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> reshaped world money, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian Exchange</a> transformed diets, populations and faiths.</li><li>Costly wars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereign-default">bankruptcies</a> eroded its power; the American colonies broke away by 1826 and the last major ones were lost in 1898.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1565 to 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila Galleon</a> linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, the first sustained direct trade link between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and Asia.</p></aside>
    </section>

