    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile.jpg" alt="Isabella I of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsabellaofCastile03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish Empire is often described as the first global empire in history, built with extraordinary speed after the dynastic union of the crowns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>, complete from 1479, and the conclusion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in January 1492. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage, sponsored by the Castilian crown and departing in August 1492, initiated the most consequential sequence of territorial acquisitions in imperial history. The empire's legal and political foundations were articulated in the papal bulls of 1493, above all <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inter-caetera">Inter caetera</a></em>, which assigned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the right to lands discovered to the west, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> (1494), which fixed the meridian dividing the Spanish and Portuguese spheres. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> (1519–1521) and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> from 1532 transformed Spain from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> colonial power into the master of the two wealthiest indigenous states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, delivering the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of the Aztec and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> treasuries into the Spanish imperial system. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles I of Spain</a>, who already held the Burgundian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, inherited Castile and Aragon in 1516 and, as Charles V, the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands and the imperial title in 1519, creating the first truly intercontinental political system in history. Under Philip II (r. 1556–1598) the empire reached its greatest extent, adding Portugal and its global network of trading posts and colonies through the dynastic union of 1580, so that the Spanish crown ruled territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Americas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the Pacific at once: the first '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-on-which-the-sun-never-sets">empire on which the sun never sets</a>.'</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/potosi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potosi.jpg" alt="Potosí" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_de_la_Villa_Imperial_de_Potos%C3%AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Parallelepiped09, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish imperial economy was built on the extraction and transatlantic transfer of American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold, which generated a fiscal windfall of unprecedented scale and at the same time created the dependency that would prove the empire's long-term economic weakness. The silver mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>), worked largely by indigenous labourers conscripted under the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a></em> draft, and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, worked mainly by indigenous wage labour, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> the source of perhaps four-fifths of the world's silver between 1500 and 1800, transforming global monetary systems and funding Spanish military ambitions across three continents. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, founded in 1503, regulated colonial trade through a monopoly that channelled American silver through a single Castilian port, generating customs revenue but also bottlenecks, smuggling and corruption, while excluding foreigners and merchants of other Spanish ports from direct trade. The flood of silver passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> into European financial systems, above all through repayments to the Genoese and German bankers who financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> campaigns, and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/price-revolution">Price Revolution</a>, a sustained sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> that damaged Spanish agriculture and manufacturing relative to northern European competitors. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a></em> system extracted labour and tribute from indigenous populations, while the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a></em> later emerged as the dominant form of landholding. The empire's commercial reach was formidable, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila Galleon</a> linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from 1565 to 1815, the first sustained direct commercial link between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and Asia, but a fiscal and mercantile policy that prioritised extraction over production hindered the development of the domestic manufacturing and banking capacity that underpinned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and British commercial wealth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi.jpg" alt="Battle of Rocroi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi">Battle of Rocroi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Bataille_de_Rocroi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sauveur Lecomte, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish military of the sixteenth century was the most formidable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, shaped by the long warfare of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, the tactical lessons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> under commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a>, 'the Great Captain', and the combination of firearms with disciplined formation warfare. Spanish infantry, victorious at Pavia (1525) and organised from 1534 into <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercios</a></em> combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> and arquebusiers, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> military dominance over its European opponents for more than a century, a reputation that was badly dented by defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi">Rocroi</a> in 1643. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the conquest of the Aztec and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> empires was accomplished not by Spanish firepower alone but through a combination of disease, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, to which indigenous Americans had no prior exposure, the exploitation of indigenous rivalries and alliances, such as Cortés's alliance with Tlaxcala, and the shock of horses, firearms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> weapons. Spanish sea power was demonstrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Lepanto</a> (1571), where Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> formed a major part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> fleet that defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Armada</a> sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1588 was defeated by English ships and wrecked by storms on the voyage home round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland. Military overextension, maintaining forces simultaneously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the Atlantic, imposed fiscal demands that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> revenues could not sustain, driving the crown into repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereign-default">bankruptcies</a>, or suspensions of payments, including those of 1557, 1575, 1596, 1607, 1627 and 1647, that undermined long-term military capacity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-flanders">Army of Flanders</a>, which fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> from 1568 to 1648, consumed resources on a scale that permanently weakened Spanish military primacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/new-laws"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-laws.jpg" alt="New Laws" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leyes_Nuevas1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan de Brocar (impresor), Jean de Vingles (grabador), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish Empire developed the first comprehensive legal and administrative framework for overseas colonial government, establishing precedents that shaped later European colonialism and, through the debates it generated, contributed to the emergence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, established in 1524, governed the American colonies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and the colonial legislation it oversaw was later gathered in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recopilacion-de-leyes-de-los-reinos-de-las-indias">Recopilación de Leyes de los Reinos de las Indias</a></em> (1680), a comprehensive code covering every aspect of colonial administration. The viceroyalties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> (1535) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> (1542) were the primary administrative units, later joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> (1717, re-established 1739) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> (1776), each headed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of high rank who exercised near-royal authority subject to the oversight of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">audiencias</a></em> (colonial high courts) and the Council of the Indies. Spanish debates over the legitimacy of conquest were unusually intense: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>'s campaign against the abuse of indigenous peoples helped produce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a>, which sought, with mixed success, to curb the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a></em> and outlaw the enslavement of indigenous Americans, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid-debate">Valladolid debate</a> of 1550–1551 he argued against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-gines-de-sepulveda">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</a>'s justification of conquest. The Church was a primary instrument of colonial rule: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patronato-real">Patronato Real</a></em> gave the crown the right to nominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and clergy in the colonies, making the ecclesiastical hierarchy an arm of the state that converted indigenous populations, educated colonial subjects and upheld social order. Corruption at every level of this complex structure was endemic, and the distances from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> meant that royal instructions often arrived months or years after the situations that prompted them had already changed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria.jpg" alt="Francisco de Vitoria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria">Francisco de Vitoria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franciscodevictoria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raúl Hernández González Seguir Francisco de Vitoria 3 Detalles de la fachada de la iglesia del convento de San Esteba..., CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish Empire's global impact was transformative on a scale unmatched by any other state of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, reshaping the demographic, ecological, economic and cultural geography of several continents at once. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian Exchange</a>, the transfer of crops, animals, pathogens and peoples between the Old World and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, was the most consequential and irreversible result of the empire's expansion. American crops including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and the sweet potato transformed nutrition across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, enabling population growth that would not otherwise have been possible; Eurasian diseases including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> killed an estimated 50 to 90 per cent of the pre-contact indigenous population within a century of contact, one of the most catastrophic demographic events in human history. American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> disrupted monetary systems from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where the Ming economy's growing reliance on silver created an enormous demand that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila Galleon</a> trade helped to satisfy. The imposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholic Christianity</a> across the Americas, through missionary conversion, institutional coercion and the destruction of pre-Columbian religious sites, permanently transformed the religious character of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> is today official in some twenty countries, a linguistic legacy of imperial rule that endures into the twenty-first century. The debates about the legal and moral status of indigenous peoples prompted by the conquest contributed to the emergence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/school-of-salamanca">School of Salamanca</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria">Francisco de Vitoria</a>, whose ideas were later developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Spanish Succession" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Final_War_of_the_Spanish_Succession_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DavidDijkgraaf, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish Empire's decline was prolonged, punctuated and asymmetric, with military and commercial primacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> lost decades before territorial contraction began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Armada</a> in 1588, while not strategically decisive in itself, marked the failure of direct maritime confrontation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the opening of northern European competition for Atlantic commercial supremacy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-munster">Treaty of Münster</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> independence after eighty years of the most expensive war in Spanish imperial history, confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s loss of hegemony in northern Europe, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Peace of the Pyrenees</a> (1659) acknowledged the ascendancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> (1701–1714) brought the Bourbon dynasty to the Spanish throne but cost Spain its Italian and Netherlandish possessions under the Utrecht settlement. The eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> sought to rationalise colonial administration, increase fiscal extraction from the Americas and build a more efficient military establishment, with partial success, as colonial revenues rose substantially, but they also deepened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> resentment that fuelled independence movements after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of Spain in 1808 displaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy and shattered the legitimacy that had held the colonial system together. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a> (1808–1826) dissolved the mainland American empire with remarkable speed, leaving Spain with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> among its remaining major colonies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a> of 1898, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> destroyed Spanish squadrons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manila-bay">Manila Bay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-santiago-de-cuba">Santiago de Cuba</a> within months, stripped Spain of these last major overseas possessions, completing an imperial dissolution that had begun nearly a century earlier, although small African territories remained. The empire's four-century duration, global reach and cultural impact nonetheless make it one of the most consequential political enterprises in human history.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Spanish Empire's supreme historical legacy is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian Exchange</a> itself, the irreversible biological, demographic and cultural transformation of the planet that followed its American conquests, making the Spanish crown the inadvertent architect of the modern world's demographic geography.</em></p>

